A||||4||Richard Parnell||clutch-the final solution||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||05-20-2000||09:31 PM||||I've tried everything on the hydraulics. The system has absolutely NO air in it.  I checked the throwout arm and as one final last gasp will crawl under the car and check that bolts for the tranny are all tight. I spoke to a Fiero mechanic in town about the situation and the conclusion is that the presure plate has crapped out which is why it probably always was a little hard to shift.  I replaced it and the clutch 3 years ago.  We decided that since I'm going to be putting in the V8 pretty soon, to not waste any more money fixing the car as it sits now.  I spent over $$00 this week working on this problem.  Hopefully I can take some of the parts back..<BR>So I'm going to buy a beater to drive to work and start working on putting in the V8.  I'll drop the cradle and start cleaning up the engine compartment and replacing suspension parts.  I'm waiting for my tax refund and that will go towards the V8 Archie master conversion kit $2900 at last check. So won't be driving my baby any more for a while.  My fingers are sore and the skin on my hands is burned from the DOT 3, also damn near blinded myself when I got tired and sloppy.  I think the mechanic's advice-to start spending towards the conversion is solid. So that's the way I'll go. Thanks everyone for your great advice.  I can now change out both the slave and master in about 20 minutes.  But that's not the problem.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||05-20-2000||09:51 PM||||Richard, I had a pressure plate collapse on a 280zx a few years ago. I did not know what happened so ended up giving the car away for half what I gave for it. Another lesson taught by stupidity, hopefully to never happed again!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||05-21-2000||03:32 AM||||My pressure plate went out too.  The parts were only $150, and if I didn't want to get all the same parts, they only would have been $80.  But it was best to have all the same.<P>Anyways, it was the installation that killed me.  $316 for installation!  Yes, I got ripped, I know now.  Anyways, $hit happens, sometimes the clutch will give out.<P>My hydraulics were fine, I had my girl's uncle check that part out.  He also moved the arm as far as it could go and the clutch still did not disengage.  So he concluded pressure plate.  And since I had to replace that, I might as well have replaced my entire clutch assembly.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||05-21-2000||10:34 AM||||Richard, I have been reading your woes, Last month had to replace my clutch and had the same problem with mine as you are having. When I pulled out the clutch I found that the fingers on the pressure plate were just about eating half way thru. Meaning that the trowout bearing had to travel much further then needed to release the clutch. Replaced it with on of my clutches and everything works just great.||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||05-21-2000||11:44 AM||||Richard - I think you're making the right choice. I hate it when I have to drive my truck to work instead of the Fiero because some job on it took longer than a weekend, so I sort of know how you feel. I think the fact that you have a v8 in your future for your car will make it worth it though.<P>Good Luck.<P>      <BR>||||reg||1
